    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe field is wired up the same way the codebase populates every other derived response field \u2014 an entity getter that MapStruct auto-maps by name into the generated `OwnerDto` (declared in `openapi.yml`), placed right next to `getDisplayName()`. That mechanism is fine and idiomatic. The problem is *which unit* the logic lives in.\n\n**`getSelfLink()` is a transport/routing concern placed in the domain entity.**\n- `Owner.java:565` hardcodes the REST URL namespace `\"/api/owners/\"` into the JPA domain model. `getDisplayName()` / `getInitials()` / salutation are genuine derivations of the owner's *own data* (name formatting) and legitimately belong on `Person`/`Owner`. `selfLink` is not owner data \u2014 it is the HTTP API's URL structure. The entity should not know how the REST layer routes to it; if the API is ever versioned (`/api/v2/owners/`) you would be editing the domain model to do it.\n- This also **duplicates the literal**: `RespondWithOwnerCreated.java:17` already builds `\"/api/owners/\" + owner.getId()` for the `Location` header. The same path string now exists in two places, and the second copy is in the layer where it actually belongs \u2014 confirming that's the right home.\n\n**Suggested placement:** compute `selfLink` during entity\u2192DTO translation, where this codebase already does its REST-boundary work \u2014 `OwnerMapper` (e.g. a `@Mapping(target = \"selfLink\", expression = ...)` or a small default/`@AfterMapping` method, with the same `id == null` guard). That keeps the `/api/owners/` route confined to the REST layer, lets the Location-header path and the selfLink share one source, and leaves the domain entity carrying only genuine domain derivations. The `openapi.yml` schema addition itself is correct and can stay.",
